'''Stacy L. Garrity''' (born May 17, 1964) is an American politician, businesswoman, and former [[United States Army Reserve]] officer who has served as the 79th [[treasurer of Pennsylvania]] since 2021. A [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]], she defeated incumbent [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democrat]] [[Joe Torsella]] in [[2020 Pennsylvania State Treasurer election|2020]] despite being outraised and trailing in polls.<ref name="Concede" >{{cite news|url=https://www.inquirer.com/politics/election/pennsylvania-treasurer-joe-torsella-loses-republican-stacy-garrity-20201110.html|title=Pa. Treasurer Joe Torsella has lost his reelection bid, in a big upset by Republican Stacy Garrity|first1=Andrew|last1=Seidman|first2=Chris|last2=Brennan|work=[[The Philadelphia Inquirer]]|date=November 10, 2020|access-date=November 10, 2020}}</ref> She won reelection in [[2024 Pennsylvania State Treasurer election|2024]], defeating Democratic challenger Erin McClelland with 51.9% of the vote.

Garrity served for 30 years in the Army Reserve, deploying three times during the [[Persian Gulf War|Gulf]] and [[Iraq War]]s and earning the nickname "The Angel of the Desert."<ref name="Angel" >{{cite web|url=https://www.npr.org/templates/story/story.php?storyId=1964381|title=The Angel of the Desert|first=Ari|last=Shapiro|work=[[NPR]]|date=June 18, 2004|access-date=November 10, 2020}}</ref><ref name="Tioga" >{{cite web|url=https://www.tiogapublishing.com/potter_leader_enterprise/iraq-war-veteran-stacy-garrity-announces-candidacy-for-pa-12/article_4c3a64f8-f7d6-535a-815b-01caa3d280e1.html|title=Iraq War Veteran Stacy Garrity announces candidacy for PA-12|work=[[Potter Leader-Enterprise]]|date=February 14, 2019|access-date=November 10, 2020}}</ref><ref name=":3">{{Cite web|last=DiStefano|first=Joseph N.|title='Desert Angel' Garrity: Pa.'s new treasurer pledges openness, close scrutiny of pension funds|url=https://www.inquirer.com/columnists/stacy-garrity-treasurer-psers-torsella-20201230.html|access-date=June 1, 2021|website=www.inquirer.com|language=en}}</ref> She retired as a colonel in 2016. She worked at [[Global Tungsten & Powders Corp.]] from 1987 to 2021, rising to vice president. In [[2019 Pennsylvania's 12th congressional district special election|2019]], she lost the Republican primary for [[Pennsylvania's 12th congressional district]] to [[Fred Keller (politician)|Fred Keller]].<ref name="Challenge" >{{cite web|url=https://apnews.com/article/4240bc6ea1be6405dd5269e696119834|title=Republican seeks party nod to run for Pennsylvania treasurer|work=[[Associated Press]]|date=January 24, 2020|access-date=November 10, 2020}}</ref> In August 2025, she announced her candidacy for governor of Pennsylvania in [[2026 Pennsylvania gubernatorial election|2026]].<ref name="Gov26">{{cite news|title=Raising Hope In Pennsylvania; Pennsylvania Treasurer Stacy Garrity is run for governor |url=https://garrityforpa.com/|access-date=August 18, 2025|date=August 18, 2025}}</ref>

==Early life and education==
Garrity was born on May 17, 1964, in [[Athens, Pennsylvania]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Stacy Garrity – Pennsylvania Treasurer candidate, Republican |url=https://www.yourerie.com/pennsylvania-politics/pa-counts/meet-the-candidates/stacy-garrity-pennsylvania-treasurer-candidate-republican/}}</ref><ref name="Wins" >{{cite web|url=https://www.pahomepage.com/news/bradford-county-native-stacy-garrity-wins-pa-state-treasurer-race/|title=Bradford County native Stacy Garrity wins PA State Treasurer race|first=George|last=Stockburger|work=[[WYOU]]|date=November 10, 2020|access-date=November 10, 2020}}</ref> She attended [[Sayre Area High School]] in [[Sayre, Pennsylvania]] and later graduated from [[Bloomsburg University of Pennsylvania]] with a degree in finance and economics, later earning a certificate from the [[Samuel Curtis Johnson Graduate School of Management|Cornell University Business Management Institute]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.weny.com/story/42892802/bradford-county-native-stacy-garrity-elected-pa-state-treasurer|title=Bradford County native Stacy Garrity elected PA State Treasurer|first=Isabel|last=Garcia|work=[[WENY-TV|WENY]]|date=November 10, 2020|access-date=November 10, 2020}}</ref>

==Military career==
[[File:Stacy Garrity US Army.jpg|thumb|Garrity in the U.S. Army]]
Garrity served in the [[United States Army Reserve]] from 1986 to 2016, reaching a final rank of colonel. While in the Army Reserve, Garrity served as a [[military police]] officer.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Republican seeks party nod to run for Pennsylvania treasurer|url=https://www.ellwoodcityledger.com/news/20200124/republican-seeks-party-nod-to-run-for-pennsylvania-treasurer|access-date=June 1, 2021|website=Ellwood City Ledger|language=en}}</ref> Garrity was deployed three times during her military career: in 1991 for the [[Gulf War]], and in 2003–2004 as well as 2008–2009 for the [[Iraq War]].<ref name=":1">{{Cite web|date=February 27, 2021|title=Meet Stacy|url=https://www.garrityforpa.com/meet-stacy|access-date=June 1, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210227004544/https://www.garrityforpa.com/meet-stacy|archive-date=February 27, 2021}}</ref>

Garrity was stationed at [[Camp Bucca]] in southern [[Iraq]] during the Iraq War. She worked to process detainees, manage family visitations, and act as a [[International Red Cross and Red Crescent Movement|Red Cross]] liaison.<ref name="Angel" /><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2003/05/06/international/worldspecial/in-victory-for-powell-bush-names-civilian.html|title=In Victory for Powell, Bush Names Civilian Overseer for Iraq|first=Joel|last=Brinkley|work=[[The New York Times]]|date=May 6, 2003|access-date=November 10, 2020}}</ref> It was here Garrity obtained the nickname "The Angel of the Desert" or "The Angel of Camp Bucca" for her affection when dealing with the prisoners housed at the camp.<ref name="Angel" /><ref name=":3" /> Garrity earned two [[Bronze Star Medal|Bronze Stars]] and the [[Legion of Merit]] before retiring in 2016.<ref name="Tioga" /><ref name="Wins" />

==Business career==
Garrity worked at [[Global Tungsten & Powders Corp.]] (GTP) from 1987 to 2021.<ref name=":2">{{Cite web|last=Corp|first=Global Tungsten & Powders|title=GTP's Vice-President Stacy Garrity elected as Pennsylvania State Treasurer|url=https://www.globaltungsten.com/en/company/news/News/detail/gtps-vice-president-stacy-garrity-elected-as-pennsylvania-state-treasurer.html|access-date=June 1, 2021|website=www.globaltungsten.com|language=en}}</ref> GTP is a producer of tungsten and metallurgic products based in Pennsylvania. Before leaving GTP to become Pennsylvania Treasurer, Garrity's final position at the company was vice president for Government Affairs and Industry Liaison.<ref name=":2" /> In 2018, while Garrity managed government affairs for Global Tungsten & Powders, she successfully lobbied Congress to ban the U.S. military from purchasing any tungsten from China, Russia, North Korea, and Iran.<ref name=":5">{{Cite web|date=October 30, 2020|title=Pa. state treasurer faces three challengers seeking his job|url=https://www.pennlive.com/elections/2020/10/pa-state-treasurer-faces-three-challengers-seeking-his-job.html|access-date=June 2, 2021|website=pennlive|language=en}}</ref>

In 2017, Global Tungsten & Powders was sued in federal court by a competitor company, Tungsten Heavy Powder and Parts, for defamation.<ref name=":6">{{Cite web|last=Malfitano|first=Nicholas|title=Defamation lawsuit involving rival tungsten product manufacturers brought to Pennsylvania federal court|url=https://pennrecord.com/stories/511268424-defamation-lawsuit-involving-rival-tungsten-product-manufacturers-brought-to-pennsylvania-federal-court|access-date=June 2, 2021|website=Pennsylvania Record|language=en}}</ref> Tungsten Heavy Powder and Parts alleged that Garrity spread defamatory statements about them at an industry event.<ref name=":6" /> The claims made by Tungsten Heavy Powder and Parts were dismissed in a court of law, and Tungsten Heavy Powder and Parts was ordered to pay over $5 million in damages.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2021-04-29 |title=Southern District of California {{!}} Tungsten Heavy Powder of San Diego Agrees to Pay $5.6 Million to Settle False Claims Act Allegations {{!}} United States Department of Justice |url=https://www.justice.gov/usao-sdca/pr/tungsten-heavy-powder-san-diego-agrees-pay-56-million-settle-false-claims-act |access-date=2023-03-25 |website=www.justice.gov |language=en}}</ref>

==Political career==
===Congressional campaign===
In 2019, Garrity ran for the Republican nomination in a [[2019 Pennsylvania's 12th congressional district special election|special election]] for [[Pennsylvania's 12th congressional district|Pennsylvania's 12th Congressional district]] after the resignation of incumbent Republican [[Tom Marino]].<ref>{{Cite web|title=PA12: Stacy Garrity Announces Bid for GOP Primary|url=https://www.politicspa.com/pa12-stacy-garrity-announces-bid-for-gop-primary/90347/|publisher=Politics PA|access-date=June 1, 2021}}</ref> Garrity lost the nomination to state representative [[Fred Keller (politician)|Fred Keller]], who was supported by president [[Donald Trump]] and eventually won the seat.<ref name=":3" /> After losing the Congressional nomination, Garrity pursued the 2020 race for treasurer.<ref>{{Cite web|date=January 23, 2021|title=Decorated Veteran, Businesswoman Stacy Garrity Announces State Treasurer Candidacy|url=https://www.garrityforpa.com/news/decorated-veteran-businesswoman-stacy-garrity-announces-state-treasurer-candidacy|access-date=June 1, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210123003432/https://www.garrityforpa.com/news/decorated-veteran-businesswoman-stacy-garrity-announces-state-treasurer-candidacy|archive-date=January 23, 2021}}</ref>

===Pennsylvania treasurer===
[[File:Dan Meuser, Matt Cartwright, and Stacy Garrity speaking to veterans, 2023.jpg|thumb|Garrity (center) with [[Dan Meuser]] and [[Matt Cartwright]] in 2023.]]

==== Comments on the 2020 presidential election ====
On multiple occasions, Garrity has supported [[Attempts to overturn the 2020 United States presidential election|Donald Trump's false claims]] that he won the [[2020 United States presidential election|2020 presidential election]]. On January 5, 2021, she spoke at a rally in [[Harrisburg, Pennsylvania|Harrisburg]] asking lawmakers to decertify Pennsylvania's election results.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Karbal |first1=Iam |title=Garrity campaign tries to walk back election denial |url=https://penncapital-star.com/election-2024/garrity-campaign-tries-to-walk-back-election-denial |access-date=December 9, 2025 |work=[[Pennsylvania Capital-Star]] |date=October 11, 2024 |location=[[Harrisburg]]}}</ref> On May 6, 2022, Garrity appeared at a [[List of post–2016 election Donald Trump rallies|rally with former president Trump]] and [[2022 United States Senate election in Pennsylvania|2022 U.S. Senate]] candidate [[Mehmet Oz]] in [[Greensburg, Pennsylvania|Greensburg]], endorsing Trump's false claim to have won the 2020 election.<ref>{{cite news |last1=McGoldrick |first1=Gillian |title=Trump takes the stage in Greensburg, with a mixed review of Oz |url=https://www.post-gazette.com/news/politics-state/2022/05/06/donald-trump-mehmet-oz-u-s-senate-save-america-guy-reschenthaler-j-d-vance-westmoreland/stories/202205060143 |access-date=May 7, 2022 |work=[[Pittsburgh Post-Gazette]] |date=May 6, 2022 |location=[[Harrisburg]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Qiu |first=Linda |date=2023-08-17 |title=Fact-Checking the Breadth of Trump’s Election Lies |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2023/08/17/us/politics/trump-election-lies-fact-check.html |access-date=2026-10-17 |work=The New York Times |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331}}</ref>

==== First term ====
Garrity challenged incumbent Democrat [[Joe Torsella]] for [[Treasurer of Pennsylvania|Pennsylvania treasurer]] in January 2020,<ref name="Challenge" /> winning the Republican nomination unopposed in June. Garrity charged that Torsella was mainly interested in the office to boost his profile and run for higher office, such as the [[List of governors of Pennsylvania|state's governorship]]. She criticized Torsella's management of the office and alleged a lack of transparency, citing a D− rating given by the [[Public Interest Research Group]] in 2019.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.fox43.com/article/news/politics/elections/candidates/pennsylvania-treasurer-election-joe-torsella-stacy-garrity/521-b77db21f-70b4-4ce7-b3e5-596ca35a1c5d|title=Pa. Treasury election candidates at odds over integrity, transparency of office|first=Matt|last=Maisel|work=[[WPMT]]|date=October 29, 2020|access-date=November 10, 2020}}</ref>

On November 10, 2020, a week after election day, Torsella called Garrity to concede the race, with Garrity leading by 78,000 votes at the time of his concession. It was the first time a Republican had unseated an incumbent Democrat for a statewide office in Pennsylvania since [[1994 United States Senate election in Pennsylvania|1994]].<ref name="Concede" />

In May 2021, Garrity joined a critical letter sent by several Republican state treasurers to the [[Presidency of Joe Biden|Biden administration]], charging that the administration was inappropriately encouraging financial institutions to stop supporting fossil fuels.<ref name=":4">{{Cite web|title=Pa. Treasurer Garrity warns banks against divesting from fossil fuel companies|url=https://www.post-gazette.com/business/powersource/2021/05/26/Pennsylvania-Treasurer-Garrity-oil-gas-coal-climate-change-banks-divest/stories/202105260150|access-date=June 1, 2021|website=Pittsburgh Post-Gazette|language=en}}</ref> The letter said that the government should not "bully corporations into curtailing legal activities," but it also threatened that the state governments represented by the signatory officials may divest their state funds from any banks that no longer support fossil fuel projects.<ref name=":4" /> Garrity's participation in this letter was criticized by an editorial in the ''[[Hazleton Standard-Speaker|Hazelton Standard-Speaker]]''.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Garrity banking on outdated energy sources|url=https://www.standardspeaker.com/garrity-banking-on-outdated-energy-sources/article_c60e73e0-5521-51a7-b69e-67c61219fe4d.html|access-date=June 1, 2021|website=Hazleton Standard Speaker|language=en}}</ref>

In honor of National College Savings Day in 2021, Garrity had the Pennsylvania Treasury offer $100 to families that opened [[529 plan|529 college savings plans]].<ref>{{Cite web|last=Walzak|first=Josh|title=State Treasurer Encourages Families to Save for Education with PA 529 Accounts|url=http://www.thecourierexpress.com/the_leader_vindicator/news/state/state-treasurer-encourages-families-to-save-for-education-with-pa-529-accounts/article_d64a8305-a588-509c-8fec-14d8e7df3feb.html|access-date=June 1, 2021|website=The Courier Express|language=en}}</ref>

In 2021, in her ex-officio capacity on the board of the Pennsylvania [[Pennsylvania Public School Employees' Retirement System|Public School Employees' Retirement System]], Garrity joined a group of "dissident" board members opposing the contemporary management of the pension fund's investments, arguing instead that the pension fund should invest in low-fee, public investments. After failing to dismiss the fund's managers, the dissident group of board members, including Garrity's predecessor Joe Torsella, successfully blocked an investment strategy proposed by the fund managers in September 2021.<ref>{{Cite web|last=DiStefano|first=Joseph N.|title=For the first time, rebels block PSERS management's investment advice at state's biggest pension fund|url=https://www.mcall.com/news/pennsylvania/mc-nws-pa-pension-fund-state-teachers-20210904-if67xrtlnfbstpq5jtstl4qblm-story.html|access-date=September 27, 2021|website=mcall.com}}</ref>

In 2022, Garrity opposed governor [[Tom Wolf]]'s proposal to award state employees with five days vacation from work if they got [[COVID-19 vaccine|vaccinated]] for [[COVID-19]], claiming that it would cost the state government 100 million dollars.<ref name="abc27.com">{{Cite web|date=February 17, 2022|title=Cost of Gov. Wolf's vaccine incentive still unclear|url=https://www.abc27.com/news/this-week-in-pennsylvania/pennsylvania-politics/cost-of-gov-wolfs-vaccine-incentive-still-unclear/|access-date=February 20, 2022|website=ABC27|language=en-US}}</ref> The Wolf administration claimed that Garrity's cost estimate was seriously exaggerated. Pennsylvania Secretary of Administration Michael Newsome, whose position reports to the governor, estimated the cost of the vacation incentives to be $45 million.<ref name="abc27.com"/>

On March 15, 2023, Garrity joined state senator [[John DiSanto]] in introducing legislation that will automatically return unclaimed property to Pennsylvania residents.<ref name="patreasury.gov">{{Cite web |title=Treasurer Stacy Garrity and Senator John DiSanto Announce Legislation to Automatically Return Unclaimed Property |url=https://patreasury.gov/newsroom/archive/2023/03-15-Money-Match.html |access-date=2023-03-25 |website=patreasury.gov |language=en}}</ref> Pennsylvania Money Match will authorize Treasury to automatically return single-owner property for living individuals valued up to $5,000 to the rightful owner after a thorough identification and verification process.<ref name="patreasury.gov"/>

In October 2023, Garrity announced that she had purchased $20 million in [[Israel Bonds]] for the state's investment portfolio, bringing Pennsylvania's investment in Israel Bonds to a total of $56 million.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Treasurer Stacy Garrity's decision to invest more Pa. money in Israel Bonds draws protest, Democratic opposition |url=https://whyy.org/articles/pennsylvania-stacy-garrity-israel-bonds-palestine-protest-treasurer-election/?utm_campaign=sproutsocial&utm_content=1708014415&utm_medium=post&utm_source=twitter |access-date=2024-08-14 |website=WHYY |language=en-US}}</ref>

==== Second term ====
[[File:Stacy Garrity inaguration, 2025 (cropped).jpg|thumb|upright=1|Garrity during her second inauguration in 2025.]]
In 2024, Garrity ran for a second term as state treasurer. She defeated Democratic challenger Erin McClelland in the [[2024 Pennsylvania State Treasurer election|general election]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://penncapital-star.com/election-2024/mcclelland-garrity-wins-race-for-pennsylvania-treasurer/|title=Garrity wins reelection as Pennsylvania Treasurer|first=Ian|last=Karbal|work=penncapital-star|date=November 6, 2024|access-date=August 2, 2025}}</ref> Garrity received more than 3.5 million votes, the most cast for any statewide candidate in Pennsylvania history.

In 2025, Garrity introduced a program offering $500 million in low-interest loans to county and local governments who had been affected by a state budget impasse, which had put local funding on hold due to the budget being three months overdue.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Pa. Treasurer Stacy Garrity expands loan program for services impacted by state budget impasse |url=https://www.newsfromthestates.com/article/pa-treasurer-stacy-garrity-expands-loan-program-services-impacted-state-budget-impasse |access-date=2025-10-06 |website=News From The States |language=en}}</ref>

Garrity is a vocal supporter of the [[One Big Beautiful Bill Act]] passed by Congressional Republicans in July 2025.<ref>{{Cite tweet |user=GarrityForPA |number=1924879933119332598 |title=As PA's Treasurer, I know firsthand how important President Trump's One, Big Beautiful Bill is for families across our Commonwealth. It's about securing our border, reducing wasteful spending, and putting money back into YOUR pockets. Congress needs to get this passed ASAP.}}</ref> The legislation cut $186 billion from the [[Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program]] (SNAP) and $1 trillion from [[Medicaid]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Local Impacts from Congress' One, Big, Beautiful Bill |url=https://www.nlc.org/article/2025/07/11/local-impacts-from-congress-one-big-beautiful-bill |website=National League of Cities |date=July 11, 2025 |access-date=January 7, 2026|language=en}}</ref>

===2026 gubernatorial campaign===
In August 2025, Garrity launched her bid for governor of Pennsylvania against incumbent [[Josh Shapiro]].<ref name="Gov26"/> President Donald Trump endorsed Garrity."<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.pennlive.com/politics/2026/01/trump-gives-republican-garrity-his-complete-and-total-endorsement-in-pa-governors-race.html|title=Trump gives Republican Garrity his 'complete and total' endorsement in Pa. governor's race|first=Amy|last=Worden|work=[[PennLive]]|date=January 28, 2026|access-date=April 15, 2026}}</ref> In March 2026, Trump hosted a fundraiser for her at [[Mar-a-Lago]].<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.pennlive.com/politics/2026/03/trump-hosts-garrity-at-mar-a-lago-fundraiser-calls-shapiro-a-stiff.html|title=Trump hosts Garrity at Mar-a-Lago fundraiser, calls Shapiro 'a stiff'|first1=Charles|last1=Thompson|work=[[PennLive]]|date=March 30, 2026|access-date=April 15, 2026}}</ref> [[Primary elections]] were held on May 19, 2026,<ref>{{Cite web|date=September 26, 2025|title=2026 State Primary Election Dates|url=https://www.ncsl.org/elections-and-campaigns/2026-state-primary-election-dates|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260509222424/https://www.ncsl.org/elections-and-campaigns/2026-state-primary-election-dates|archive-date=May 9, 2026|access-date=September 27, 2025|website=[[National Conference of State Legislatures]]}}</ref> in which Garrity won the primary uncontested.<ref>{{cite web|last=Fink|first=Jenni|date=May 19, 2026|title=Stacy Garrity’s Chances of Defeating Shapiro After Pennsylvania Primary Win|url=https://www.newsweek.com/stacy-garrity-chances-defeating-josh-shapiro-pennsylvania-governor-11967772|access-date=May 21, 2026|work=[[Newsweek]]}}</ref>

